1.4 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you reached the Terminal in RStudio Server and know it is not the Console;
session_01_commands.sh is saved, includes the commands that failed, and runs from an empty folder;
pwd ends in bioinfo_course/session_01/data, and that folder contains mystery.fasta;
you can say the file has 40 records, that grep -c "^&gt;" is the reliable way to count them, and why wc -l gives a different number;
you can name the record that is not DNA and the record that was truncated, and say how you spotted each;
you can explain in one sentence that a FASTA record starts on a line whose first character is &gt;.

2.4 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can name the four lines of a FASTQ record and say what line 4 encodes;
you can read a !/#/,/:/F/I character as roughly bad-to-excellent without recomputing the formula;
../results holds three FastQC reports, produced by a fastqc command you ran yourself;
you can point to one plot in a report and say, in one sentence, why it does or does not worry you;
you made a trust/flag decision on an unseen sample, backed by two named observations.

3.4 Checkpoint
You are done when all of the following are true:

you can say what pident, evalue, and bitscore each roughly mean, and can name a real case from today where they did not all agree;
you ran a strict and a sensitive BLAST search and can explain why they gave different numbers of hits;
you built a tree yourself with FastTree and can say what its support values roughly mean;
you can explain, in your own words, how an alignment turns into a tree, and read a distance matrix as a table;
you fetched real sequences from NCBI yourself, built a 114-tip tree from them (including unknown_sample), and can say where the named variants sit relative to Wuhan-Hu-1 and to each other;
you can say where unknown_sample sits in that tree, and why that is independent confirmation of blocks B-D2’s conclusion;
you re-rooted that tree on Wuhan-Hu-1 and can explain both why the named variants’ groupings did not change, and why that reroot gave a less clean picture than rooting on unknown_sample;
you compared a nucleotide-level and a protein-level percent identity for the same gene pair;
you have a complete draft interpretation in WORKSHEET.md, ready to polish and submit by Thursday.
